Stay at the requirements level: *what* we are building and *why*. Defer *how*
to the design stage.

**Surface your assumptions.** Anything the request leaves ambiguous, state as
an explicit assumption under Open Questions rather than silently filling it in.
Assumptions are the most dangerous form of misunderstanding, and this document
exists to expose them before code is written.

**Reframe vague requirements as testable conditions.** "Make it faster" becomes
a target someone can check — a number, a threshold, a specific observable.

Cover these sections. Drop one only when it genuinely does not apply, and say so
rather than leaving it blank:

```markdown
# Spec: <feature name>

## Objective
What we are building and why. Who the user is. What success looks like.

## Tech Stack
Framework, language, and key dependencies with versions, as the repository
actually uses them.

## Commands
Full executable commands with flags — build, test, lint, dev — not tool names.

## Project Structure
Where source, tests, and docs live in this repository.

## Code Style
One real snippet from this codebase plus the naming and formatting conventions
it shows.

## Testing Strategy
Framework, where tests live, coverage expectations, which level of test covers
which concern.

## Boundaries
- Always: ...
- Ask first: ...
- Never: ...

## Success Criteria
Specific, testable conditions that decide whether this is done.

## Open Questions
Anything unresolved that needs human input, including your assumptions.
```

